WEBVTT

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:05.470
Attention.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.029
How a language model decides what matters.

00:00:08.629 --> 00:00:10.520
I keep the lamp at Night School.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.666
Tonight the lamp is the lesson.

00:00:13.866 --> 00:00:15.975
Here is a sentence with a hole at the end.

00:00:16.575 --> 00:00:18.227
Rerun found a kettle.

00:00:18.547 --> 00:00:20.873
Pylon put a cone on the...

00:00:21.473 --> 00:00:22.995
What word goes in the hole?

00:00:25.495 --> 00:00:27.147
You said kettle.

00:00:27.467 --> 00:00:29.619
And you did not read the sentence again.

00:00:29.939 --> 00:00:34.026
You glanced back, past the full stop, to one word near the start, and took it.

00:00:35.226 --> 00:00:37.813
A language model like this one does one job.

00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:41.611
It reads the words so far and guesses the next one.

00:00:42.211 --> 00:00:49.602
The first neural translation models squeezed the whole sentence into one fixed-length list of numbers, and worked from that.

00:00:50.202 --> 00:00:52.637
Think of a message whispered down a line of people.

00:00:52.957 --> 00:00:55.870
By the end of a long line, the message has worn thin.

00:00:56.470 --> 00:01:06.340
In 2014, Bahdanau, Cho and Bengio let a translation model look at every word of the sentence while it wrote each new word, and weigh how much each one counts.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.616
They called it an alignment model.

00:01:08.936 --> 00:01:11.001
Today we call the idea attention.

00:01:12.201 --> 00:01:14.267
Here is how the looking works.

00:01:14.587 --> 00:01:16.565
Every word does three things.

00:01:17.165 --> 00:01:18.513
It asks a question.

00:01:18.833 --> 00:01:20.311
It holds up a label.

00:01:20.631 --> 00:01:22.500
And it carries some cargo.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.600
The word at the end shines its question back along the sentence.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.464
Each earlier word answers with its label.

00:01:31.064 --> 00:01:33.042
A good match gets a high score.

00:01:33.362 --> 00:01:36.558
The scores are turned into shares that add up to one hundred percent.

00:01:37.158 --> 00:01:43.571
Then the word takes a blend of the cargo of every word so far, itself included, in those shares.

00:01:43.891 --> 00:01:46.152
That blend is what it carries forward.

00:01:47.352 --> 00:01:49.091
Let's go inside the lamp.

00:01:49.691 --> 00:01:52.452
Every word here is a list of 32 numbers.

00:01:52.772 --> 00:01:54.489
One dot is one number.

00:01:55.089 --> 00:02:01.458
A match is a dot product: multiply the two lists number by number, and add it all up.

00:02:02.058 --> 00:02:09.189
Each head works on a shorter list of its own, 16 numbers here, and the score is divided by the square root of that length.

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:16.422
Without it, long lists give huge scores, the shares go all or nothing, and the model learns slowly.

00:02:17.022 --> 00:02:20.283
This is a real model, trained on sentences from our coast.

00:02:20.603 --> 00:02:25.798
It has 20,864 numbers in total, and nothing in it was set by hand.

00:02:26.398 --> 00:02:29.355
Which earlier word will get most of the light?

00:02:31.855 --> 00:02:37.529
On this sentence, the word at the end gives more than 99 percent of its light to kettle.

00:02:38.729 --> 00:02:41.185
One beam asks one kind of question.

00:02:41.505 --> 00:02:45.288
A transformer runs several at once, and calls each one a head.

00:02:45.888 --> 00:02:50.888
The base transformer in the 2017 paper had eight heads in every attention layer.

00:02:51.208 --> 00:02:53.230
The larger one had sixteen.

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:56.721
Ours has 2 heads in each of 2 layers.

00:02:57.041 --> 00:02:59.063
Nobody told them what to look for.

00:02:59.663 --> 00:03:04.706
After training, a head in the second layer puts 71 percent of its light on the word to be copied.

00:03:05.306 --> 00:03:13.675
In small attention-only models, researchers at Anthropic found pairs of heads, in different layers, that work together to copy.

00:03:13.995 --> 00:03:17.452
The first writes into each word a note about the word before it.

00:03:17.772 --> 00:03:23.837
The second uses that note to find an earlier copy of the current word, and reads the word that followed it.

00:03:24.157 --> 00:03:25.962
They called them induction heads.

00:03:27.162 --> 00:03:29.140
Here is the test that matters.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:34.982
We trained the same model again with the lamp turned off, so each word could see only itself.

00:03:35.582 --> 00:03:40.321
With attention, it filled the copy holes correctly 99 percent of the time.

00:03:40.921 --> 00:03:44.768
With each word seeing only itself, 17 percent.

00:03:45.368 --> 00:03:46.651
Then one more run.

00:03:46.971 --> 00:03:52.384
Every word got an even share of light over everything before it, with no choosing.

00:03:52.704 --> 00:03:55.030
That run reached 64 percent.

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:57.956
Knowing which words came before helps.

00:03:58.276 --> 00:04:01.233
Choosing where to look is what finishes the job.

00:04:02.433 --> 00:04:03.476
There's a bill.

00:04:03.796 --> 00:04:07.470
Every word compares itself with every word before it.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:12.570
In each head, a thousand words means about half a million comparisons.

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:16.542
Double the text, and the attention work goes up about four times.

00:04:17.142 --> 00:04:20.642
That's why people build special machinery to do this fast.

00:04:20.962 --> 00:04:24.114
We take it apart in the cannery, in a later lesson.

00:04:25.314 --> 00:04:27.271
One warning before you go.

00:04:27.871 --> 00:04:30.936
The light shows where a head is reading from.

00:04:31.256 --> 00:04:35.126
It does not, on its own, tell you why the model answered what it did.

00:04:35.726 --> 00:04:43.421
In 2019, researchers showed that in some text classifiers, very different attention patterns can give almost the same answer.

00:04:43.741 --> 00:04:48.415
Others replied that it depends on what you mean by explain, and proposed tests.

00:04:48.735 --> 00:04:51.540
Treat the light as a clue, and check it.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.174
The lamp room is open below this film.

00:04:55.494 --> 00:04:59.038
Train the model yourself, and watch the heads learn where to look.

00:04:59.638 --> 00:05:02.920
My voice is synthesized, made from the author's voice.

